\section{Experiments and Results}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:methodology}

\paragraph{Base architecture.}
All experiments build on DreamerV3 \citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, which learns an RSSM with discrete latent states ($32$ categories $\times$ $32$ classes), uses symlog-transformed predictions, and trains an actor-critic entirely in imagined trajectories. The default configuration imagines $H{=}15$ steps at $\dt{=}1$ (one imagination step per environment transition).

\paragraph{Variable-rate imagination.}
\label{sec:variable_rate}
We introduce three orthogonal modifications enabling variable-rate imagination, each independently toggled.
\emph{Temporal embedding}: A learned embedding $e(\dt)$ is concatenated to the RSSM input at each imagination step. We test three modes: \textbf{trained} (updated via backpropagation, enabling co-adaptation), \textbf{frozen} (fixed random projection, \texttt{requires\_grad=False}), and \textbf{removed} (dimension zero). We further distinguish \textbf{trained-and-exposed} (subsample ON: the RSSM trains on $\dt{>}1$ transitions) from \textbf{trained-but-unexposed} (subsample OFF: $\dt{>}1$ used only during imagination).
\emph{Temporal subsampling}: When enabled, the replay buffer augments training data with variable-$\dt$ transitions by subsampling consecutive frames (max factor~2).
\emph{Horizon and step size}: We test $H{=}15$ (default) and $H{=}8$; \texttt{gate\_fixed\_dt} forces a specific $\dt$ ($1$, $2$, or $4$).

\paragraph{Factorial design.}
\label{sec:factorial}
We adopt a factorial ablation across four factors: $\dt$-embedding mode (trained / frozen / none), temporal subsampling (on / off), imagination horizon ($H{=}15$ / $H{=}8$), and step size ($\dt{=}1$, $2$, $4$). The design (12 conditions, Table~\ref{tab:factorial_design} in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}) isolates trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate exposure as orthogonal factors. Key configurations use 4--5 seeds; following \citet{agarwal2021precipice}, we report per-seed trajectories.

\paragraph{Diagnostic metric.}
\label{sec:metrics}
We define the normalized value-to-eval ratio $\nve_t = (v/e)_{\text{config},t} / (v/e)_{\text{baseline},t}$, where $v/e$ is the critic's mean imagined value divided by mean evaluation return, and the baseline is the highest-performing seed (exp\_001, seed~1, 505K steps). Analysis is restricted to a \textbf{35K+ window} to avoid early-training inflation. The $\nve \geq 2.0\times$ threshold classifies all $n{=}36$ configurations across two environments (AUROC${=}1.000$ [95\% bootstrap CI: 1.000--1.000], F1${=}1.000$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_sensitivity}).

\paragraph{Intervention methods.}
\label{sec:sgs_method}
\emph{Selective gradient stopping} (SGS): $e_{\Delta t}$ is detached via $\operatorname{sg}(\cdot)$ before selected prediction heads during the backward pass. We test reward-only (exp\_016e), full-pathway (exp\_018), and obs-decoder-only (exp\_025/026) variants. \emph{TCN} adds Gaussian noise $\mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$ to $e_{\Delta t}$ during imagination ($\sigma \in \{0.2, 0.5\}$, three environments); \emph{RAVE} clips imagined values exceeding an EMA anchor ($c \in \{2.0, 1.5\}$). \emph{Dual-critic}: a $2{\times}2$ factorial of \{single, dual\} $\times$ \{narrow (${\sim}1.2$M), wide (${\sim}2.4$M)\} critic following TD3 \citep{fujimoto2018td3}.
\label{sec:forward_pass_method}
\label{sec:dual_critic_method}

\paragraph{Environments and training.}
\label{sec:env_details}
All experiments use Walker Walk and Cheetah Run from DMC \citep{tassa2018dmcontrol} with proprioceptive observations. Walker Walk (6-joint biped) is the primary environment; Cheetah Run (6-joint runner) serves as a severity probe (CIVO at $8$--$70\times$ NVE vs.\ $2$--$5\times$ on Walker). Baselines run 500K steps; treatments 105K steps. Hyperparameters follow DreamerV3 defaults \citep{hafner2023dreamerv3} ($d{=}32$ embedding; Appendix~\ref{sec:hyperparameters}). Training uses a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU (${\sim}80$ GPU-hours total). The codebase extends the official DreamerV3 implementation\footnote{\url{https://github.com/danijar/dreamerv3}, accessed March 2026.} with the variable-rate modules described above.

%% ============================================================
%% RESULTS
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F1: All Variable-Rate Configurations Degrade}
\label{sec:results}
\label{sec:f1}

Every configuration that introduces $\dt > 1$ imagination degrades relative to the $\dt{=}1$ baseline (Figure~\ref{fig:learning_curves}). The baseline reaches 915.7 at 105K; fixed $\dt{=}2$ (006) peaks at 485.3 before collapsing to 132.9 ($14\%$ of baseline); fixed $\dt{=}4$ (007) never exceeds 176.9 ($19\%$); the learned gate (005) collapses to 98.8 ($11\%$).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_learning_curves.pdf}
\caption{Learning curves. The baseline (black) reaches 915.7; all variable-rate configurations degrade.}
\label{fig:learning_curves}
\end{figure}

%% ============================================================
%% TABLE 1: Main results summary
%% ============================================================

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Key configurations and outcomes. NVE reports peak $\nve$ in the 35K+ window (\S\ref{sec:metrics}). CIVO-positive seeds are shaded. Complete results for all 43 configurations in Table~\ref{tab:complete_results}.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{llcccccl}
\toprule
